# Binary assets, declared rather than detected.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is normal on Windows checkouts of this repo, and git's own
# heuristic — a file containing a NUL byte is binary — does keep these intact; that was
# measured before this file was written, by comparing each font's sha256 on disk against
# the blob git produced for it. This file exists so the answer does not depend on a
# heuristic and on whatever `core.autocrlf` a future contributor happens to have.
#
# The failure it prevents is silent in the worst way: a woff2 with its 0x0A bytes rewritten
# to 0x0D0A still has a `wOF2` signature, still has a plausible size, still passes
# `is_file()`, and simply fails to decode in the browser — which falls back to Palatino
# without a word, exactly the way the missing font did for weeks before it was noticed.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
